<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikephoros II Phokas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 912–<a href="/years/969">969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Nicephorus II Phocas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikephoros II Phokas was a Byzantine general and emperor from 963 to 969. He reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in 961 after the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Chandax</a>, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 962 and later took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. A patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanasios-the-athonite">Athanasios the Athonite</a>, he backed the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lavra">Great Lavra</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>. He was murdered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John Tzimiskes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas.jpg" alt="Nikephoros II Phokas" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Byzantine artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nikephoros_Phokas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Nicephorus II Phocas</b> retook Cilicia from the Arabs in 965 the Byzantines settled Christians from Syria and Armenia there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperors of the 10th century protected these holdings: Constantine VII forbade selling military land below a value of four pounds of gold for a cavalryman and two for a sailor, and <b>Nikephoros II Phokas</b> raised the minimum to twelve pounds for his new heavy cavalry.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The general <b>Nikephoros II Phokas</b> was crowned emperor, swearing to protect the two sons of the late Romanos II, Basil II and Constantine VIII, who were already co-emperors.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-16">16 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantine emperor <b>Nikephoros II Phokas</b>, who had retaken Crete and Cyprus and conquered Cilicia, was murdered by his nephew, the general John Tzimiskes, who seized the throne as John I.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-11">11 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Co-emperor in name since 962, alongside his father Romanos II, <b>Nikephoros II Phokas</b>, John I Tzimiskes and his brother Basil II, Constantine VIII became sole emperor on Basil's death.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-15">15 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As Byzantine power revived in the 10th century, <b>Nikephoros II Phokas</b> sent the patrician Niketas Chalkoutzes to retake Cyprus in 965.</blockquote><a href="/state/arab-byzantine-condominium-of-cyprus">Arab-Byzantine Condominium of Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
